[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done. Two files changed:

  [1m[38;5;153mclient.py[0m — added [38;5;153m_resolve_verify()[39m and a module-level [38;5;153mVERIFY[39m, passed as [38;5;153mverify=[39m on both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls:
  - default: [38;5;153mTrue[39m (full verification — this is what [38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m gets)
  - [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE=/path/to/cert[39m → verifies against that CA file
  - [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY=0[39m → verification off

  [1m[38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[0m — sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY=0[39m via [38;5;153msetdefault[39m, with a comment pointing at the CA-bundle route as the
  better fix.

  Verified: resolution logic returns [38;5;153mTrue[39m by default, [38;5;153mFalse[39m under [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY=0[39m/[38;5;153mFalse[39m, and the path when
  [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m is set (tested with a stubbed [38;5;153mrequests[39m — the module isn't installed here, so no live request was
  made). [38;5;153mruff check[39m and [38;5;153mruff format --check[39m pass on the changed files. Nothing committed.

  Two things worth knowing:

  - [38;5;153murllib3[39m will now print an [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m on each staging call. I left it unsuppressed deliberately — it's
    the visible marker that the connection is unverified. Say so if you want it silenced.
  - [38;5;153mruff check scripts/[39m flags pre-existing [38;5;153mRUF100[39m unused-[38;5;153mnoqa[39m warnings on the [38;5;153m# noqa: E402[39m import lines in both scripts.
    Unrelated to this change, so I left them alone.

  If you ever want staging verified properly, export the self-signed cert to a file and set [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m instead of
  [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY=0[39m — the client already supports it.

